{
  "concept": "A Fibonacci heap is a circular doubly linked list of min-heap-ordered trees; the abstract tree picture and the explicit pointer picture describe the same structure, and the pointer view is what makes insertion O(1).",
  "figureType": "network_diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel label '(a)' at left of the upper view",
    "Panel label '(b)' at left of the lower view",
    "H.min label with a downward arrow pointing at root node 3, present in both panels",
    "Root list of five roots, left to right: 23, 7, 3, 17, 24",
    "Panel (a) dashed horizontal line connecting the five roots to indicate the root list",
    "Tree rooted at 3 with children 18, 52, 38 (solid edges)",
    "Node 18 with single child 39",
    "Node 38 with single child 41",
    "Tree rooted at 17 with single child 30",
    "Tree rooted at 24 with children 26 and 46",
    "Node 26 with single child 35",
    "Trees rooted at 23 and 7 are single nodes with no children",
    "Marked nodes 18, 39, 26 drawn as filled black circles with white numerals",
    "Unmarked nodes drawn as light-gray circles with black numerals and thin black outline",
    "Panel (b): circular doubly linked root list rendered as two horizontal rails above and below the roots with paired forward (rightward) and backward (leftward) arrows, wrapping around at both ends",
    "Panel (b): each sibling/child list (18-52-38, 30 alone, 26-46, 39 alone, 41 alone, 35 alone) drawn as its own circular doubly linked list with wrap-around rails",
    "Panel (b): vertical paired arrows between a parent and one of its children representing the parent pointer (child→parent) and the child pointer (parent→child)",
    "Panel (b): self-looping rails on singleton lists showing a lone node's left and right pointers point to itself",
    "Legend: gray = unmarked node, black = marked node (lost one child since becoming a child)",
    "Highlight overlay labels used on click: 'p (parent)', 'child', 'left', 'right'",
    "Insertion overlay: new node circle with editable key, dashed splice arrows into the root list, and 'O(1)' annotation"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Representation",
      "options": ["(a) abstract trees", "(b) pointer structure"],
      "default": "(a) abstract trees",
      "teaches": "The tidy tree drawing and the tangle of arrows are the same heap; the dashed root line and the parent-child edges are shorthand for circular doubly linked lists and parent/child pointers."
    },
    {
      "id": "inspectNode",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Every node stores exactly four pointers — p, child, left, right — and in a sibling list of size one the left and right pointers point back at the node itself."
    },
    {
      "id": "insertPhase",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "FIB-HEAP-INSERT(H, 21)",
      "states": ["before", "new node created", "spliced into root list", "H.min updated", "done"],
      "default": "before",
      "teaches": "Insertion only relinks a constant number of pointers in the root list and compares one key against H.min — no tree restructuring, so it costs O(1)."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The abstract view",
      "narration": "Five min-heap-ordered trees sit in a root list, drawn as a dashed line; H.min points at the smallest root, 3. Each tree obeys the heap property, but nothing constrains the trees' shapes relative to one another.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(a) abstract trees", "insertPhase": "before" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Marked nodes",
      "narration": "Nodes 18, 39, and 26 are black: each has lost one child since it last became the child of another node. A second loss would cut it into the root list.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(a) abstract trees", "insertPhase": "before" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The same heap, in pointers",
      "narration": "Switch to the pointer view: the dashed root line becomes a circular doubly linked list, and every set of siblings gets its own circular list. Click any node to light up its parent, child, left, and right pointers.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(b) pointer structure", "insertPhase": "before" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why circular and doubly linked",
      "narration": "Click node 30 or 35: a lone child's left and right pointers loop back to itself. Circular lists mean splicing a node in or out touches only a fixed number of pointers, with no head or tail special cases.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(b) pointer structure", "insertPhase": "before" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Insert key 21",
      "narration": "Create a single-node tree holding 21 and splice it into the root list between two existing roots by rewiring four pointers.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(b) pointer structure", "insertPhase": "spliced into root list" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Check H.min",
      "narration": "Compare 21 against H.min's key 3. Since 21 > 3, H.min stays on 3. The whole operation was a constant number of pointer writes and one comparison — O(1), with no consolidation.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(b) pointer structure", "insertPhase": "H.min updated" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Back to the abstract picture",
      "narration": "Return to the tree view: the heap now has six roots. The lazy, unconsolidated root list is exactly what buys Fibonacci heaps their fast inserts and decrease-keys.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "(a) abstract trees", "insertPhase": "done" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Because the root list and every sibling list are circular and doubly linked, adding a tree is just four pointer assignments and one key comparison — the heap stays deliberately messy so that insertion costs O(1).",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two stacked panels sharing identical node positions. Nodes are circles ~28px radius: unmarked = light gray fill, thin black stroke, black numeral; marked (18, 39, 26) = solid black fill, white bold numeral. Panel (a): five roots evenly spread across the top on one horizontal line (23, 7, 3, 17, 24) joined by a black dashed line; H.min rendered as italic serif text above node 3 with a short downward arrow. Solid thin edges descend from 3 to its three children (18, 52, 38 spread beneath it), from 18 to 39, from 38 to 41, from 17 to 30, from 24 to 26 and 46, and from 26 to 35. Depth increases downward in three levels. Panel (b): same node coordinates, but each sibling group is wrapped in a rounded 'racetrack' rail — one rail above the group carrying rightward arrows, one below carrying leftward arrows, with the rails curving around the outermost nodes to close the cycle. Singleton lists (30, 39, 41, 35) get small self-loop rails above and below. Between a parent and its leftmost-drawn child, draw a pair of parallel vertical arrows in opposite directions (child→parent for p, parent→child for child pointer); other children connect to the parent only via the sibling rail. Use a single accent color (e.g. orange) for click-highlighted pointers with small italic labels p / child / left / right, and a second accent (e.g. green) for the newly inserted node 21 and its dashed splice arrows. Keep the layout wide and airy; the visual contrast between the calm dashed line of (a) and the dense arrow lattice of (b) is the point."
}

